<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">New Caledonia</h1><p class="st-meta">1853 CE – present · capital Noumea</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A French Pacific territory annexed in 1853, rich in nickel and long divided over independence between Kanak and settler communities.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> named the main island in 1774, and Catholic and Protestant missions followed in the 1840s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed New <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-caledonia-darien">Caledonia</a> in 1853 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>, partly to establish a penal colony. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noumea">Port-de-France</a>, founded in 1854, was renamed Nouméa in 1866.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Between 1864 and 1897 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> transported about 22,000 convicts, among them deported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communards">Communards</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louise-michel">Louise Michel</a>. Nickel was discovered in 1864 and mining became the core of the economy. The indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanak-people">Kanak</a> were confined to reserves under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigenat">indigénat</a> code, and they rose in major revolts in 1878, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atai">Ataï</a>, and in 1917. The colony rallied to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-france">Free France</a> in 1940 and was a major American base in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>It became an overseas territory in 1946 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanak-people">Kanak</a> gained citizenship. Violent conflict in the 1980s ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matignon-accords-1988">Matignon Accords</a> of 1988 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noumea-accord">Nouméa Accord</a> of 1998. Referendums in 2018, 2020 and 2021 rejected independence, and deadly unrest broke out in 2024 over voting rights.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/new-caledonia.jpg" alt="New Caledonia" width="320" height="180"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Caledonia_in_France_(zoomed).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1853 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Noumea</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism, Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Kanak languages</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nouvelle-Calédonie</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1940&amp;focus=new-caledonia" aria-label="New Caledonia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="9138 3000 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where New Caledonia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M9458 3256L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M9458 3256h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M9458 3256h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Noumea. <a href="/atlas?year=1940&amp;focus=new-caledonia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

